<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chant des Partisans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">melody <a href="/years/1941">1941</a>; whistled as a BBC signature tune from 17 May <a href="/years/1943">1943</a>; French words 30 May <a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Le Chant des partisans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;Chant des Partisans&quot;, also called the &quot;Chant de la Libération&quot;, was the best-known song of the Free French and the French Resistance during the German occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its music is by Anna Marly, a Russian émigrée who had left France for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1940 and served there as a volunteer canteen worker with the Free French Forces.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>Chant des Partisans</b>&quot;, also called the &quot;Chant de la Libération&quot;, was the best-known song of the Free French and the French Resistance during the German occupation of France.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chant-des-partisans">Chant des Partisans</a></li></ul></section>
